To determine the minimum energy of gamma rays whose lifetime in the universe is limited by the process \(\gamma\gamma \rightarrow e^+e^-\) with the CMB photons, we need the center-of-mass energy of the colliding photons to be at least the rest mass energy of the electron-positron pair, which is \(2m_e c^2 \approx 1.022 \text{ MeV}\).

The CMB photons have an average energy of \(E_{\text{CMB}} \approx 10^{-3} \text{ eV}\). For a high-energy gamma ray of energy \(E_\gamma\), colliding with a CMB photon of energy \(E_{\text{CMB}}\), the center-of-mass energy squared in the lab frame is approximately (for non-relativistic CMB photons compared to the gamma ray):

\[
s \approx 4 E_\gamma E_{\text{CMB}}
\]

We require:

\[
4 E_\gamma E_{\text{CMB}} \geq (2m_e c^2)^2
\]

Solving for \(E_\gamma\):

\[
E_\gamma \geq \frac{(2m_e c^2)^2}{4 E_{\text{CMB}}}
= \frac{(2 \times 0.511 \text{ MeV})^2}{4 \times 10^{-3} \text{ eV}}
= \frac{(1.022 \text{ MeV})^2}{4 \times 10^{-3} \text{ eV}}
\]

Convert units: \(1 \text{ MeV} = 10^6 \text{ eV}\), so

\[
(1.022 \times 10^6 \text{ eV})^2 = 1.044 \times 10^{12} \text{ eV}^2
\]
\[
4 \times 10^{-3} \text{ eV}
\]
\[
E_\gamma \geq \frac{1.044 \times 10^{12} \text{ eV}^2}{4 \times 10^{-3} \text{ eV}} = \frac{1.044 \times 10^{12}}{4 \times 10^{-3}} \text{ eV}
= \frac{1.044 \times 10^{12}}{4 \times 10^{-3}} \text{ eV}
= \frac{1.044 \times 10^{12}}{4} \times 10^{3} \text{ eV}
= 0.261 \times 10^{15} \text{ eV}
= 2.61 \times 10^{14} \text{ eV} = 2.61 \times 10^{5} \text{ GeV}
\]

So the answer is approximately \(2.6 \times 10^5\) GeV.

Answer: C
